\title{qqrtools: Tools for Quantile-on-Quantile Regression}
\description{
The \strong{qqrtools} package provides tools for estimating and analysing
\emph{quantile–on–quantile regression} (QQR) surfaces. QQR extends standard
quantile regression by modelling the response at multiple conditional
quantile levels of a \emph{quantile regressor}, evaluated over a grid of
values of that regressor. This produces a surface of coefficients
indexed by quantile of the regressor (grid) and conditional quantile
level (tau).
}
\details{
This package implements:
\itemize{
\item Local, kernel-weighted quantile regressions along a grid of the
quantile regressor.
\item Storage of estimated coefficient and p-value surfaces for the
intercept, the quantile regressor, and control variables.
\item Consistent user-facing naming of coefficients (no internal
aliasing).
\item Tools to compare QQR surfaces with standard quantile regression
(QR), including row means of QQR surfaces.
\item Flexible extraction and export utilities (wide/long surfaces,
Excel, Word).
\item Static and interactive 3D visualisations of surfaces.
}
\subsection{Key Features}{
\subsection{Model Estimation}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{qqr()}}: Fit a QQR model given a formula, data frame, and
user-specified quantile regressor (\code{qvar}). The function returns
an object of class \code{"qqr"} containing coefficient and p-value
surfaces, grid values, quantile indices (\code{taus}), and the data
used in the fit.
\item Data used in the original fit is stored in the object (\code{fit$data})
to enable robust post-estimation comparisons and exports, avoiding
scoping issues when re-evaluating calls.
}
}

\subsection{Naming Conventions}{
\itemize{
\item Coefficients are named using \strong{user-facing variable names}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"(Intercept)"} for the model intercept (if present)
\item The original \code{qvar} name for the quantile regressor
\item Control variables as given in the formula
}
\item The internal local regressor (“z = x − z0”) is \strong{never exposed} in
public surfaces or comparisons.
}
}

\subsection{Comparison with Standard Quantile Regression}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{compare.qqr()}}: Compares the row means of a QQR coefficient
surface with coefficients from standard quantile regression
(\code{quantreg::rq}). For each coefficient of interest, it returns
a data frame with columns:
\itemize{
\item \code{tau}: quantile level
\item \code{qr}: coefficient from standard QR
\item \code{qqr_mean}: row-mean of the QQR surface
}
\item Invalid coefficient names (e.g., internal aliases like \code{"z"}) are
rejected with informative errors.
\item Multi-coefficient comparisons (e.g., \code{coef = c("x", "z1")}) return
a named list of comparison data frames.
}
}

\subsection{Surface Extraction}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{qqr_surface()}}: Extract wide or long format tables for
coefficient and p-value surfaces.
\item Allows subsetting by grid points and quantile levels (\code{taus}).
\item Supports optional rounding for display and export.
\item \strong{\code{rowave.qqr()}}: Computes row means across quantile levels for
a specified coefficient or all coefficients, with tidy labelling.
}
}

\subsection{Export Utilities}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{excel.qqr()}}: Writes coefficient or p-value surfaces to an
Excel workbook (one sheet per coefficient), with grid values and
optional rounding.
\item \strong{\code{word.qqr()}}: Writes the same to a Word document with formatted
tables (requires \code{officer} and \code{flextable}).
}
}

\subsection{Static and Interactive Plotting}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{plot.qqr()}}: Static 3D visualisation of coefficient or p-value
surfaces using multiple engines:
\itemize{
\item \code{"base"} — built-in R graphics
\item \code{"plot3D"} — enhanced 3D visualisation if installed
}
\item \strong{\code{Int.plot()}}: Interactive 3D plots (requires \code{rgl}).
